  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "A quadratic equation \\(ax^2-2ax+b=0\\) has two real solutions. What is the average of the solutions?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{b}{a}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{2b}{a}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\sqrt{2a-b}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "a",
    "value": "1"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2008-p9.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "By Vieta’s formulas, the sum of the roots of \\(ax^2-2ax+b=0\\) is \\(\\dfrac{-(-2a)}{a}=2.\\)\n\nThe average is half of this, namely \\(1.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "For \\(Ax^2+Bx+C=0,\\) the sum of the roots is \\(-\\dfrac{B}{A}\\)",
        "The average is half the sum of the roots"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
